            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "**How to use the mole concept?**",
            "start": 1441,
            "end": 1473
          },
          {
            "id": "p13",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Let's consider an example:",
            "start": 1475,
            "end": 1501
          },
          {
            "id": "p14",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Suppose you have 1 mole of carbon atoms (C). The molar mass of carbon is 12 g/mol, which means that 1 mole of carbon atoms weighs 12 grams.",
            "start": 1503,
            "end": 1642
          },
          {
            "id": "p15",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Using Avogadro's Number, we know that 1 mole of carbon contains 6.022 x 10^23 carbon atoms.",
            "start": 1644,
            "end": 1735
          },
          {
            "id": "p16",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Now, let's say you want to find the number of atoms in 2 moles of carbon. You can simply multiply the number of moles by Avogadro's Number:",
            "start": 1737,
            "end": 1876
          },
          {
            "id": "p17",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "2 mol x 6.022 x 10^23 atoms/mol = 1.2044 x 10^24 atoms",
            "start": 1878,
            "end": 1932
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The molar mass of carbon (12 g/mol) and the multiplication (2 mol * 6.022 x 10^23 atoms/mol = 1.2044 x 10^24 atoms) are completely accurate.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
